---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import StaticCode from "../../components/StaticCode.astro";
import { siteData } from "../../data/data";

const toc = [
  { id: "what", label: "What is verified" },
  { id: "results", label: "Results" },
  { id: "matrix", label: "Profiles × targets" },
  { id: "negative", label: "Negative cases" },
  { id: "performance", label: "Performance" },
  { id: "limits", label: "What did not run" },
];

const report = siteData.conformance;
const passedCases = report.cases.filter((item) => item.reference.status === "passed").length;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Verification"
  description="The exact evidence behind h#: positive cases, negative cases, profile equivalence, four real toolchains, and measured editor latency."
  active="/docs/verification"
  toc={toc}
  prev={{ href: "/docs/targets", label: "Targets" }}
  next={{ href: "/docs/language", label: "Back to language core" }}
>
  <h2 id="what">What is verified</h2>
  <p>
    Verification is not the reference interpreter agreeing with itself. Every positive case has an
    expected value or error code written independently in the conformance manifest. Each case is
    then checked through:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>The reference interpreter.</li>
    <li>Structural AST equivalence under every profile.</li>
    <li>Exact keyword round-trip preservation.</li>
    <li>Real compiled execution on C#, Rust, Go, and Java.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Overflow cases pin the failing source node, so a backend cannot emit a generic
    <code>node: 0</code> and still pass. Generated programs must exit 0 for success and non-zero for
    a program failure.
  </p>

  <h2 id="results">Results</h2>
  <div class="proof-strip" style="margin:1.2rem 0;">
    <div class="proof-item"><strong>{report.summary.cases}</strong><span>positive cases</span></div>
    <div class="proof-item"><strong>{report.summary.targets}</strong><span>target executions</span></div>
    <div class="proof-item"><strong>{report.summary.failed}</strong><span>failed</span></div>
    <div class="proof-item"><strong>{report.summary.blocked}</strong><span>blocked</span></div>
  </div>
  <p>
    Latest report: <code>{report.generatedAt}</code>. Mode: <code>{report.mode}</code>. The
    reference outcome matched expectations in <strong>{passedCases}</strong> positive cases.
  </p>
  <StaticCode code={`npm test

# or
hs test --all-targets`} language="bash" title="reproduce" meta="one command" />
  <p>
    Reports are written to <code>reports/conformance.json</code> and
    <code>reports/conformance.md</code>. The JSON includes every profile/target row and all negative
    diagnostics.
  </p>

  <h2 id="matrix">Profiles × targets</h2>
  <p>
    Four profiles multiply the target checks: standard, my-syntax, terse, and casual. Structural
    equivalence is verified separately, then each compiled artifact is executed once per profile.
    The same artifact is used because generated code is profile-independent.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Target</th><th>Toolchain</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {
        report.toolchains.map((tool) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{tool.name}</td>
            <td><code>{tool.version}</code></td>
            <td>{tool.available ? "available" : "blocked"}</td>
          </tr>
        ))
      }
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Use the <a href="/playground">conformance explorer</a> to filter the real case list by target,
    status, or name.
  </p>

  <h2 id="negative">Negative cases</h2>
  <p>
    The suite also rejects invalid programs and profiles. Negative cases assert the exact diagnostic
    code, not merely that something failed:
  </p>
  <div class="row">
    {
      report.negative.map((item) => (
        <span class:list={["chip", item.status === "passed" ? "chip-lime" : "chip-java"]}>{item.id}</span>
      ))
    }
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:1rem;">
    Covered diagnostics include wrong argument types and counts, unknown names, missing returns,
    direct and indirect recursion, positive minimum-literal rejection, chained comparisons,
    non-Boolean conditions, operator type errors, redeclarations, duplicate functions and
    parameters, syntax errors, and seven profile-validation failures.
  </p>

  <h2 id="performance">Performance</h2>
  <p>
    The editor pipeline is measured separately from correctness. On the verification machine
    (Node 24.21, Windows x64), the warm analyze + four-target generation path is sub-millisecond for
    normal files, and the persistent bridge round-trip stays in the low milliseconds.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Measurement</th><th>Observed</th><th>Budget</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Analyze 246-character document</td><td>0.03–0.09 ms</td><td>2 ms</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Analyze 500 functions (57 KB)</td><td>15–35 ms</td><td>150 ms</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Generate all four targets, small</td><td>0.04–0.27 ms</td><td>8 ms</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Analyze + generate all four</td><td>0.05–0.27 ms</td><td>25 ms</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Bridge round-trip incl. C#</td><td>0.4–3 ms</td><td>30 ms</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Reproduce with <code>npm run bench</code>; raw data is in <code>reports/benchmark.json</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="limits">What did not run</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Interactive Visual Studio Code UI behavior was not driven by an automated UI test; the extension is typechecked, packaged, and installed.</li>
    <li>Cross-platform execution was not verified; toolchains ran on Windows x64.</li>
    <li>No broad performance claims, memory profiling, or target build-time benchmark.</li>
    <li>No committed property-based fuzzing or incremental-parser campaign.</li>
    <li>The POC does not establish correctness for concurrency, broad ecosystem interoperability, or application-level performance.</li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
